  "imports/loc.huge_name": {
   "cite": "activerecord-4.2.11.3/CHANGELOG.md:1788 -- \"PostgreSQL and SQLite string columns no longer have a default limit of 255.\" (Fixes #13435, #9153.) Implementation: activerecord-4.2.11.3/lib/active_record/connection_adapters/postgresql_adapter.rb:82 `string: { name: \"character varying\" }` against activerecord-4.0.0/lib/active_record/connection_adapters/postgresql_adapter.rb:396 `string: { name: \"character varying\", limit: 255 }`.",
   "obs": {
    "aborted": false,
    "exception": null,
    "exit_ok": true,
    "stdout": [],
    "task": "import:locations[<FIXTURES>/huge-name.csv]"
   },
   "why": "rake import:locations on a CSV whose name column is 4000 characters. The schema declares `t.string \"name\"` with no limit. On 4.0 the PostgreSQL adapter gave :string a default limit of 255, so the column was character varying(255) and the insert raised ActiveRecord::StatementInvalid; the task aborted with a non-zero exit. On 4.2 :string has no default limit, the column is an unbounded character varying, and the same row is accepted -- the import succeeds and exits 0. The flip is a property of the column the schema now creates, so it is visible to any tree that loads this schema on 4.2, whatever the application does."
  },
  "migrate/migrate.status_before": {
   "cite": "activerecord-4.2.11.3/lib/active_record/railties/databases.rake:99-101 -- `unless ActiveRecord::SchemaMigration.table_exists?; abort 'Schema migrations table does not exist yet.'; end` against activerecord-4.0.0/lib/active_record/railties/databases.rake:93-96, which is `puts 'Schema migrations table does not exist yet.'` followed by `next  # means \"return\" for rake task`.",
   "obs": {
    "aborted": false,
    "exception": null,
    "exit_ok": false,
    "stdout": {
     "no_table": false,
     "rows": []
    },
    "task": "db:migrate:status"
   },
   "why": "rake db:migrate:status against a database that has no schema_migrations table. On 4.0 the task printed \"Schema migrations table does not exist yet.\" to stdout and used `next` to return from the task, so the rake process exited 0. On 4.2 the same condition calls `abort`, which writes the message to stderr and exits non-zero. The exit status and the stream the message is on both change; the task body is the framework's own railtie rake file, which the application does not define and cannot override without redefining the task."
  }
